      "question": {
        ":question/id": "ac69d200-8444-588b-8337-fc94521a654a",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\ln(- 2 x)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Here $f(x)=\\ln(- 2 x)$ is a function of a function. We use the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\ln u,\\,u=- 2 x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{- 2 x}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ &=\\frac{-2}{- 2 x} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\cancel{-2}}{\\cancel{-2}x} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
